# Interface-First Development

When implementing complex, multi-component systems, always follow an interface-first approach:

1. Define public trait interfaces before implementing any concrete types
2. Document parameter types, order, and return values explicitly
3. Create stub implementations that satisfy interfaces before building full implementations
4. Write integration tests that verify compatibility between components
5. Use compiler warnings as indicators of interface misalignment

This approach prevents issues like parameter order mismatches (as seen below) and ensures components can be integrated with minimal friction:

```rust
// AVOID: Inconsistent parameter order
trait AssessmentEngine {
    fn assess(&self, document: &Document, framework: &Framework) -> Result<Assessment>;
}

impl AssessmentEngine for CustomEngine {
    // Parameters don't match trait definition!
    fn assess(&self, framework: &Framework, document: &Document) -> Result<Assessment> {
        // ...
    }
}
```

By defining interfaces first, you'll prevent integration errors at compile time, enable parallel development with clear contracts, and facilitate easier testing.
